{"tag":"GYXY","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>There is a finite number of income levels, and an income distribution gives the proportion of individuals at each level, so it has the mathematical structure of a lottery. Consider the relation “one distribution is more egalitarian than another”.</p></div><ol class=\"pq-parts\" role=\"list\"><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (a)\">a</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Why is the von Neumann–Morgenstern independence axiom inappropriate for characterizing this type of relation?</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Independence is inappropriate because mixing two perfectly equal distributions creates inequality. With two income levels <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"a≠ b,\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord katex-vbox\"><span class=\"katex-thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"katex-fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> an egalitarian ranking has <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"[a]∼[b],\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∼</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> yet mixing each half and half with <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"[b]\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span></span> yields the unequal <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"0.5[a]⊕0.5[b]\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0.5</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⊕</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.5</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span></span> and the equal <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"[b],\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> which it cannot rank as equally good, as independence would require.</p></div></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (b)\">b</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Suggest and formulate a property that is appropriate, in your opinion, as an axiom for this relation. Give two examples of preference relations that satisfy it.</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Our answer: mixture quasi-convexity. A mixture of two distributions is never strictly preferred to both of them.</p></div></div></div></li></ol>"}